.TH CHTTP_DEFAULT_CLIENT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_default_client \- give the default HTTP client of the process
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

chttpcli chttp_default_client(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function gives the default client of the process, which the
library initializes only when it first needs it. The function is
thread-safe, and the library makes only one such client for each
process.

The default client uses the default settings: pool_size is the CPU
count, there are no timeouts, and TLS verification is on. You can
configure it with the same chttpclient_set_* functions as any other
client.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The handle of the default client, or CHTTPCLI_INVALID if the
initialization fails.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Do not give this handle to chttpclient_destroy. This module owns the
default client and destroys it automatically at the exit of the
process, from a handler that it registers with atexit(3) the first
time that it builds the default client; a call of chttp_default_client
from an exit handler that runs after that one gives CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
If you do destroy it, that chttpclient_destroy call does not crash,
because the module knows the handle and clears its own reference to it;
but the library never builds the default client again. Every later call
to chttp_default_client, chttp_do, chttp_get and the other wrappers in
the process then fails cleanly and runs no request, for the rest of the
life of that process. If you need a client whose life you control, make
your own client with ccol_create_chttpclient or with
ccol_create_chttpclient_mp.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_create_chttpclient (3),
.BR chttpclient_destroy (3),
.BR chttp_do (3),
.BR chttp_get (3)
